WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.320
The Flash’s second trailer is building&nbsp;
the hype and we’ve got a breakdown of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.660
the good and the bad of how the fans are reacting!

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:11.940
Ezra Miller's Barry Allen may or may not have&nbsp;
a future in James Gunn and Peter Safran's DCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:17.100
following "The Flash" star's string of arrests and&nbsp;
assorted controversies. Gunn's monumental praise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.580
for "The Flash" indicates that regardless&nbsp;
of Miller's behind-the-scenes troubles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.760
the film is nothing short of&nbsp;
a complete and utter delight.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.620
“I completely broke the universe”

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.900
"The Flash" is set to premiere on&nbsp;
June 16, and thanks to CinemaCon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.400
people who aren't directly affiliated with the&nbsp;
studio have finally gotten a peek at the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.020
If their first reactions are anything to go&nbsp;
by, "The Flash" is one intriguing piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.640
superhero cinema. Critic and journalist Scott&nbsp;
Menzel tweeted this after seeing the film:

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.020
"The Flash is hands down one of the best&nbsp;
superhero films of all time. No joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.880
The Flash is the ultimate movie going experience&nbsp;
as it has a little bit of everything! Action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.600
emotion, heart, humor and plenty of nostalgia.”

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.500
He also praised stars Miller,&nbsp;
Michael Keaton, and Sasha Calle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.400
as well as the movie's considerable&nbsp;
rewatch potential. Menzel also made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.120
a point of noting that the movie has&nbsp;
plenty of extremely spoiler-y surprises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.260
which writer Nicola Austin mentioned in&nbsp;
her Twitter analysis, as well, sharing:

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:18.360
"The Flash is a compelling, character-driven flick&nbsp;
with a real emotional core &amp; game changing stakes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.680
With heart &amp; humour aplenty – along with&nbsp;
some shocks &amp; surprises – there's so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.140
to be excited for. Keaton steals the show with a&nbsp;
terrific performance. A real love letter to DC!"

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.100
The majority of the first reactions from&nbsp;
CinemaCon shower "The Flash" with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.720
extremely high praise. Fandango’s Erik Davis&nbsp;
thinks “The Flash” might actually be one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.340
very best superhero movies out there. He ranted&nbsp;
on Twitter about his love for the film, saying:

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:47.160
"DC's The Flash is TREMENDOUS! Forget DC, it is&nbsp;
without a doubt among the best superhero films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:53.640
ever made. An all-timer. Inventive storytelling,&nbsp;
FANTASTIC action sequences, great cast. SO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.560
MANY nerdy details. I'm in tears at the end.&nbsp;
Everything you want from a superhero film &amp; more."

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.740
He also compared the movie to the&nbsp;
time-travel classic "Back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.220
Future." Eric Goldman of Fandom also&nbsp;
tweeted good things about the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.912
praising "The Flash" for its&nbsp;
creative decisions and sense of fun.

00:02:08.912 --> 00:02:10.800
“You’re strapped to your parachutes.”&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.916
“Where’s yours?!”

00:02:13.096 --> 00:02:16.038
“Whooo!”

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.980
Of course, not everyone who's had a chance&nbsp;
to watch "The Flash" is entirely enamored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:23.760
with it. However, DC fans should still&nbsp;
be pretty excited by the fact that even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.660
the harsher critics are quick to admit&nbsp;
that the movie is fun time. Andrew J.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.960
Salazar of Discussing Film wrote&nbsp;
his opinions on Twitter, saying:

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.220
"The Flash manages to deliver glorious&nbsp;
high thrills. Andy Muschietti captures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.800
what many of us have always&nbsp;
dreamed for a cinematic Flash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.060
story. But there's also plenty of just&nbsp;
*bizarre* decisions. Some story related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.240
and others technical. Not amazing&nbsp;
but still pretty good at times."

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.840
It's worth noting that all this praise&nbsp;
comes with the movie still missing one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.980
big piece - the hype-building post credits&nbsp;
scene. Eric Goldman explained on Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:57.600
that he expects a different closing when the&nbsp;
film actually goes to open in theaters. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.380
that said – "The Flash" packs plenty of&nbsp;
punch in its own right ... and the studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:05.460
might still opt to add a game-changing&nbsp;
stinger or two as the icing on the cake.

